Tauri `bundle.externalBin` staging directory — WINDOWS ONLY.

The Recall SDK sidecar launcher `recall-desktop-sdk-<target-triple>.exe` is
built into this directory by `sidecar/recall-sdk-bridge/build.mjs` (a Node
Single Executable Application — a real compiled PE32+) and referenced by
`tauri.windows.release.conf.json`'s `bundle.externalBin`. It is a build
artifact and is not committed.

macOS deliberately ships NO externalBin. Tauri places externalBin entries in
`HQ.app/Contents/MacOS/`, which makes them nested code objects. This directory
used to hold three *bash script* wrappers (`recall-desktop-sdk-*-apple-darwin`)
for exactly that purpose. Because a script is not a Mach-O, `codesign` cannot
embed its signature and stores it in extended attributes instead
(`com.apple.cs.CodeDirectory`, `com.apple.cs.CodeSignature`, …). The Tauri
auto-updater extracts `HQ_x.y.z_universal.app.tar.gz` with a tar that does not
preserve xattrs, so every auto-updated install lost those signatures and failed
`codesign --verify --deep --strict` ("code object is not signed at all"). macOS
will not persist TCC privacy grants against an invalid bundle, so Accessibility
and Screen Recording never stuck and meeting detection could never start.

macOS/Linux now spawn `node <Resources>/recall-sdk-bridge/bridge.mjs` directly
(`hq_desktop_core::recall_sdk::resolve_sdk_command`). Plain files under
`Contents/Resources/` are sealed by content hash in
`_CodeSignature/CodeResources`, carry no xattrs to lose, and survive the
updater's extraction intact.

DO NOT add a non-Mach-O executable here for a macOS target. The
`recall-sidecar-bundle` e2e spec fails the build if you do.
